{"kind":"notes","id":"nt/gjalmn","slide":6,"text":"4 minutes. The slide intentionally has no visible question. Point once to the whole stack, then to the single black band: this course does not build an entire DBMS. Follow the magnification into query, plan, and work. Ask verbally: Why does our scope reach down into the data representation? Take at most two answers. Accept row or column layout, indexes, ordering, hashing, size, and statistics. Conclude exactly: We study how a query becomes executable work, but that work depends on what the data looks like. Transition: now you are the query processor.\n\n[Sources]\n- https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/query-path.html\n- https://www.postgresql.org/files/developer/tour.pdf\n- https://dev.mysql.com/doc/dev/mysql-server/latest/PAGE_SQL_Optimizer.html\n- https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.0/en/execution-plan-information.html\n- https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.0/en/innodb-architecture.html\n- /Users/stargazermiao/workspace/join-processing-datalog/doc/notes/syllabus.md\n- /Users/stargazermiao/workspace/join-processing-datalog/doc/notes/project.md\n[/Sources]"}